WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:07.370
NEIL MUELLER: Hi, my name is
Neil Mueller with Google Cloud

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:08.270
Platform.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:11.150
We're here as part of
the On-air Cloud Series.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.090
It's a series of live
webinars for Google Cloud.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:16.070
Today, we're hosting
two webinars--

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.114
my webinar on pricing,
and then, Sergei and team

00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:20.030
will join me after five
minutes-- five minutes

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.910
when I'm finished-- to talk
about streaming analytics.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.980
Also, next month, something
to look forward to,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.150
we'll be talking about security
and IT development tools.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.570
Without further ado, let's talk
about customer-friendly GCP

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.220
pricing.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.550
I've got a lot of
ground to cover today.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.900
But let me spend a few
minutes on why we're here.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.620
We're here because customers are
really excited about the cloud.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.360
But unfortunately, a
number of customers

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.820
feel like they're wasting money.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.430
Now, fortunately, most of these
cloud customers are not ours.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.060
And we'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.540
But according to RightScale,
which is a third party analyst

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.915
firm, they wrote a report
called "The State Of The Cloud"

00:00:55.915 --> 00:00:57.170
in 2017.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.930
And they said that 45% of
their cloud spend was wasted.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.069
This is self-reported waste.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.890
So you want to believe it.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.560
On the left hand side,
you see the cause

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.450
of a lot of this waste.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.540
We've got inflexible three year
VM leases that are provided

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.360
by other cloud providers.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.080
And flexible VM
configurations, where

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.190
you have to buy a
certain amount of RAM

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.630
to get a certain amount of CPU--

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.280
also something not on our
cloud, but on every other cloud.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.190
And per-hour billing, as
opposed to what we have in here

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.610
in GCP, which is
per-minute billing.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.200
So customers are feeling like
they're wasting a lot of money

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.280
on the cloud at the
same time that they're

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.230
saving a lot of money,
because the cloud is

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.900
much less expensive than
on-prem alternatives.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.000
We're here to give you
the best of both worlds,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.430
to give you a cloud that is
exactly what you expected

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.530
to pay for with
customer-friendly pricing

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.606
models that you can
really easily understand.

00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:50.730
So this is a summary slide.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.795
We'll come to it at
the very end, also.

00:01:52.795 --> 00:01:55.170
If you look on the left, we've
got other cloud providers.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.510
And let's consider that
a baseline of 100%, what

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.130
you would expect to pay.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.540
On the right hand
side, we've got

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.550
the discounts-- the
discount innovations

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.989
that we have here at GCP.

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:05.530
And we'll talk
through each of those.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.259
So an average of 24% savings
from sustained use discounts.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.870
This is very similar
to the card that you

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.120
get when you go to your
favorite sandwich shop.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.980
And if you buy 10
sandwiches, they

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.680
give you one for free--
sustained use discount.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.380
When you use a lot of GCP,
you save a lot on GCP.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.590
21% list price differences.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.020
This is just the rack
rate of our compute.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.120
If you look at
the list price, we

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.531
are, on average, 20% less
than other cloud providers.

00:02:32.531 --> 00:02:34.530
And I'll talk to you a
bit more in a few minutes

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.600
about why that's the case.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.630
15% average savings from
rightsizing recommendations.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.740
We recommend within
the cloud console,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.747
if you're using too much
or too little compute,

00:02:45.747 --> 00:02:47.580
we recommend that you
can either save money,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.850
or that you should
beef up that server

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.820
to make it more performant.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.020
On average, if you add
those numbers together,

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.710
you get a 60%
average discount when

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.880
you compare us to other clouds.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.870
That's a big discount.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.260
All right, let's talk about
all of our pricing innovations.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.847
This slide is very much a
table of contents for my talk.

00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:11.430
We're going to start
in the upper left

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.450
and work our way down
to the lower right.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.230
So let's talk about
list price advantages.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.560
I've talked to you about how
we get a 21% average list price

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.280
reduction compared
to other clouds.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.470
Sustained use discounts--
the lunch counter card--

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.660
up to 24% average savings.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.930
Committed use discounts,
up to 57% savings

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.140
when you buy
instances in advance,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.700
when you commit to their use.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.250
Permanent billing,
up to 38% savings

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.820
when you pay per minute
and not per hour.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.820
Custom machine types,
up to 19% savings.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.806
Because if you need a lot
of RAM but not a lot of CPU,

00:03:48.806 --> 00:03:51.550
you can only pay for
exactly what you want.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.260
We call that custom
machine types.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.000
Rightsizing recommendations,
15% average savings.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.480
We give you recommendations
on whether you

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.690
need more CPU or more RAM.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:06.600
Preemptible VM instances,
we call these PVMs.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.560
And we give you an average
18% savings on workloads

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.270
that can be interrupted.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.230
Network service tiers,
our newest addition--

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:15.600
we just launched it last week.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.300
It allows you to either pick
performance or cost savings

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.730
when you buy your
network from us.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:26.210
Coldline, archival storage
at the speed of tape--

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.770
the speed of disk
and the cost of tape.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.930
And lastly, support.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.870
We offer you the opportunity
to pay per user per month,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.320
as opposed to a flat percentage
fee uplift on your cloud cost.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.730
Let's talk about why GCP is
the perennial price leader.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.970
Well, it has a lot to
do with our volume.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:50.890
Google has a lot
of infrastructure.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.170
You've probably used
YouTube and Google Search.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.540
You may have also used
Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.220
We are a very large
technology company

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.840
with a lot of infrastructure.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.570
We buy in bulk, we purpose build
much of our infrastructure.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.660
We spent more last year than
any other cloud provider--

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.570
$10.9 billion in capex in 2016.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:12.350
Google's vast scale means that
we can offer you more for less.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.070
We also are smart about how
we build our infrastructure.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:17.890
On average, a Google data
center uses 50% less energy

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.390
than a typical data center.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:23.050
Our PUE, which is a
measure of our efficiency--

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.260
our power usage
effectiveness-- is 1.12,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.480
lower than any other
public cloud provider.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.350
This means that we use
less energy than average.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.570
If you look at the global
average of the largest data

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.820
centers in the
world, they are 1.7.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.000
Now, what this means
is that about 40%

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.310
of the average energy
in a data center

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.430
is wasted to cooling, or
heating, or just general loss.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:48.220
By comparison, about 10% of
the energy in a Google center

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.320
is wasted, meaning
90% of the energy

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.899
that we buy goes to
providing you cloud services.

00:05:52.899 --> 00:05:54.940
We're more efficient when
we run our data centers

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.760
and we can pass the
savings on to you.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.560
We're also more
efficient, because we

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.960
deploy very interesting
technologies like machine

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:03.720
learning.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:04.640
You may have seen
the article written

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.130
about DeepMind, who applied
some of their technology

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.272
to our data center.

00:06:08.272 --> 00:06:09.980
And if you look on
this graph right here,

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.820
time is on the x-axis.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.750
On the y-axis is PUE, this
power use effectiveness

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.580
ratio that I told you about.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.030
The closer you are
to 1, the better.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:23.280
Google is closer to 1 than
other average data centers.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.640
And when we deployed DeepMine's
AI in our data center,

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:28.140
it reduced cooling by 40%.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.940
So you can imagine what
this ML looks like.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.190
Let's take, for example,
we've got, like, 10 dials.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:37.220
And each dial has
10 positions on it.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.470
That's 10 to the 10th
power, or 10 million.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.680
That's a lot of instances
to calculate through,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.930
which is exactly what DeepMind
is calculating through,

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.180
and finding the exact
right middle ground where

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.040
we can safely operate
our data centers,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.127
and also simultaneously
reduce cooling.

00:06:51.127 --> 00:06:53.210
This allowed us to reduce
the cooling bill by 40%.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.330
Were not the only one noticing.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.770
Greenpeace gave us an A. Other
cloud providers in the space

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.740
didn't get an A. We are in
this as a result of energy

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:10.090
transparency, committing to
being 100% renewable by 2017.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:15.150
And our green
advocacy that we do.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.150
We think our data centers are
beautiful inside and outside.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.757
And in fact, this is one
of our murals that we have.

00:07:20.757 --> 00:07:22.840
If you send me a Tweet and
you're the first person

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.370
to send it to me, I'll send
you a $25 Starbucks gift card.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.890
This is part of
our mutual project.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.934
We paint many of our
data centers with murals.

00:07:31.934 --> 00:07:33.600
Just send me the state
name via Twitter,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.560
and I'll send you
that gift card.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.420
This is a very
large data center.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.700
All right, let's talk about
sustained use discounts.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:45.450
This is the sandwich car
that I talked to you about.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:49.840
So on the x-axis here, we have
the monthly average usage.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:54.460
And so if you use 100% of
this VM for the entire month,

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.115
you can get a 30% savings.

00:07:58.115 --> 00:07:59.740
We're seeing our
customers experiencing

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.710
an average of 24% average
savings, because many

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.156
of our customers use that
VM for the entire month.

00:08:05.156 --> 00:08:07.530
In other words, they're getting
a lot of free sandwiches.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.270
What sustained use provides
is if you use us more,

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:14.670
we will give you a lower price.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:15.870
This is automatic.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.440
There's no upfront
payments required.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.850
There's no lock in, and there's
no complex decision making.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.499
You don't even have to remember
the sandwich card for us

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:24.540
to give you the discount.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.110
So it really is just that easy.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.650
You might be
wondering, we use terms

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.030
like customer-friendly pricing.

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:34.530
But are we really
that friendly when

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.840
we're doing the calculation
of sustained use discounts?

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.429
I can tell you, yes we are.

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:41.370
If you look at the
graph on the lower left,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.140
you can imagine in
the actual use case

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.010
where you've got a
whole bunch of VMs

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:48.690
and a whole bunch
of projects across

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.690
your entire organization,
what we could do

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.320
is we could not be
friendly about it.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.570
And we could calculate
your actual usage

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:01.770
and say that you hadn't used
a single VM the entire month.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.300
But that's not how
we calculate it.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.280
What we do is we
have this technique

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.012
that we call inferred
instances, where

00:09:07.012 --> 00:09:08.970
if you look in the middle,
where we group them,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.039
we group all of the
instance usage together.

00:09:11.039 --> 00:09:13.080
And then, on the far right,
we compute a discount

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:14.200
based on that.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:17.792
So we put them
together in a way that

00:09:17.792 --> 00:09:20.250
looks like a very successful
"Tetris" game, which gives you

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.037
the maximum possible
sustained use discount, truly

00:09:23.037 --> 00:09:24.120
customer friendly pricing.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.630
Let's talk about CUD,
Committed Use Discount.

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.260
What this is, is when you
buy cores and memory in bulk.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.630
You can either select to use
a one year or a three year

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:37.410
commitment.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.050
And you can change your
machine types at any time.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.840
If you do this with us for
one year for three years,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.390
we'll give you up
to a 57% discount--

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:45.950
big discount.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.440
So what you're doing here is
you're buying scores and memory

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.970
in bulk.

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:56.330
If you buy it for one
year, it's a 37% savings.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:10:01.340
If you buy it for three
years, it's a 57% savings.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:04.130
If you compare this to other
similar offerings in the cloud

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:04.850
space--

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.000
for example, AWS's
reserved instances--

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.670
this is, again, a report from
a company called RightScale.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.110
They're a third party
advisory firm out there.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.526
And this is a report that they
did very recently in March,

00:10:15.526 --> 00:10:17.930
when we released
Committed Use Discounts.

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.727
We are the blue block.

00:10:19.727 --> 00:10:21.560
In this case, lower is
better, because we're

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.540
talking about price and costs.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:27.360
So because of the
rigidity of the AWS RIs,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.870
and the flexibility of the
committed use discounts,

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.750
the realized benefit
for you is much higher.

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:34.730
In this case, the
costs are much lower.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.820
Let's get into a bit more detail
about Committed Use Discounts.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.900
Like I said, these are
available in one and three year.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.700
You commit to a
certain number of vCPUs

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.540
in a certain amount of memory.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.990
This is valid for any
Google Cloud Compute Engine,

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:57.160
or GKE non-shared core VMs.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:59.920
And it's ideal for predictable
steady state workloads.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:09.320
I said that it's a committed
use on CPU and also memory.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.750
There are very flexible
limits on the ratio

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.700
between memory and CPU.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:17.780
So anything from 0.9 gigabits
of RAM up to 6.5 gigabytes

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:19.160
of RAM per CPU--

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.600
inclusive those two numbers--

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.040
is an appropriate ratio.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.770
So if you've got a very
memory-intensive application

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:27.270
that doesn't require much
CPU, committed use discounts

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.650
is still going to
be good for you.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.790
The discounts apply to
aggregate regional usage.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.210
For example, of your usage in
Iowa, or Oregon, or London.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.950
This is a summary slide talking
about Committed Use Discounts.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:45.149
What I want to highlight here
is the very far right block,

00:11:45.149 --> 00:11:47.440
which says that in order to
attain these deep discounts

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.530
without upfront payment, you
can attain these deep discounts

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:53.170
without upfront payments at
all, because we don't require

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.700
you to do any upfront payments.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:58.309
This is very different from
other reserved instance-type

00:11:58.309 --> 00:12:00.100
structures that you
see there in the cloud.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:03.810
Per-minute billing.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:10.000
So, on GCP, you only
pay for what you use.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.870
So for example, if you're
using a VM for only 11 minutes,

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:15.437
you're going to pay for 11
minutes, not 60 minutes.

00:12:15.437 --> 00:12:17.520
This is a big differentiator
with from other cloud

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.750
providers.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.297
Let's walk through
a short example.

00:12:22.297 --> 00:12:24.130
So on the right here,
you see that if you're

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:28.040
using the virtual machine for
between 12 and 25 minutes,

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.630
your average savings
is up to 38%.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.040
Custom machine types.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:39.400
If you go with another
cloud provider,

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:42.880
you will be dictated a certain
amount of CPU and memory.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.140
And if you need more
CPU or less CPU,

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.630
you have to pay for the
stuff that you don't use.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.310
So right here on
the x-axis, you've

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.720
got a series of machines.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.370
And the gray would be waste.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.940
In this case, on GCP, the
waste would be much, much less.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.360
On average, we're seeing
customers save up to 19%

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:05.810
when they use custom
machine types.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:09.040
This is a screenshot that I
took from the user interface.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:12.370
We've got these dials where you
can dial up the amount of cores

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.170
or the amount of
memory that you want.

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:15.850
And we did the math,
and it turns out

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:19.380
that there are 695
different compute types.

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:21.380
If you compare that to
other cloud providers,

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.050
we have 110 more compute
types than they do.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:31.010
So you get exactly what
you want and nothing more.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:32.760
Rightsizing recommendations.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.650
This is very similar to
a custom machine types.

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:38.690
What this looks like
in the user interface

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.290
is a recommendation
where it says,

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.530
hey, you're using
a bit more instance

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:45.260
compute than you have purchased.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:46.640
Would you like to upgrade?

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.751
Or in more cases than that, you
have purchased an instance type

00:13:49.751 --> 00:13:52.250
that is larger than you need
and you're paying for something

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.240
that you're not using.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.340
Would you like to save
yourself a little money?

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:58.070
And all you have to do is
click the button right here

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.200
to either dismiss all of
those recommendations,

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:01.520
or to learn more.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.950
And with one click,
you can activate them.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:05.510
It's the opposite
of requiring you

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:08.150
to plan in advance for demand
that you can't possibly

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:08.650
forecast.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:18.360
25% of monitored instances get
a recommendation on average.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:19.310
So that's a lot.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.380
It'll be a good portion
of your instances.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:25.520
Now, you can very easily
dismiss these or take action.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:29.960
Recommendations take a
look at CPU and memory.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.260
On average, we're seeing 60% of
those recommendations offering

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:35.390
to save you money,
and 40% offering

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:38.736
