more traffic than you then you

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.130
may have expected.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:47.210
If you use stack
driver, the granularity

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.440
of these recommendations
improves quite a bit.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:56.350
Let's talk about
preemptible machine types.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:59.740
Preemptible machine types
are very good for workloads

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:04.380
such as batch or scientific,
predictable workloads

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:06.130
that you don't think
are going to go down.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:08.980
They're exactly
like regular VMs,

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:12.190
except for they are
80% less expensive.

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:15.370
They can run for a maximum
of 24 hours, at which time,

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:17.800
they'll be shut down.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.940
You can terminate them
earlier if you like.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:23.417
And it offers
predictable billing

00:15:23.417 --> 00:15:25.000
compared to other
cloud providers that

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.920
have similar
structures like this,

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:29.099
but they're based
on marketplaces--

00:15:29.099 --> 00:15:30.640
where you would have
to hire somebody

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:33.220
to go into that marketplace
and understand the flow

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.110
price of that machine instance.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.440
Whereas here, it's just a
flat 80% off, so much easier

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.240
to forecast.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:42.420
We view this as
customer-friendly pricing.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.990
Like I said, they're very good
for various kinds of workloads

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:52.100
that you know might be
able to be interrupted.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:53.600
For example, batch
jobs, pipeline

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:56.890
builds, after hours processing,
big data workloads, analytics,

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.300
media and coding, scientific
work, financial modeling,

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:03.582
dev test, crawling,
that kind of workloads.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:10.800
What is most amazing about PVMs
is how easy they are to deploy.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.329
I mentioned with other
types of structures

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:15.870
like this from other
cloud providers,

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.786
you might need to hire
somebody to go in there

00:16:17.786 --> 00:16:20.130
and understand the
marketplace dynamics

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:22.260
of their interruptible
instances.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.640
Not with ours.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.924
They are a flat 80%
price reduction.

00:16:25.924 --> 00:16:27.840
And you can either
activate it using this code

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.239
right here that I've got on
the screen, or in the cloud

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:31.530
console with a point and click.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:36.030
Customers love these.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:40.170
For example, Mark Johnson,
the founder of Descartes Labs,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:43.410
has been able to predict
corn yield more accurately

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.660
than the United States
Department of Agriculture, all

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:46.920
using PVMs.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.810
So he's benefiting from
GCP at 80% the cost

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:53.314
and producing incredible
output for his customers.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:57.900
Network service tiers.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:00.480
Last week, we became the
first major public cloud

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.760
to offer a tiered cloud network.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.990
What this means for you
is that you can either

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.230
choose to pick performance,
which we call the Premium

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:10.905
Tier--

00:17:10.905 --> 00:17:13.300
think premium gasoline.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:16.800
If you pick the Premium Tier,
it is on average, 70% faster

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.810
than other cloud networks.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:22.829
It is 14% to 80% more expensive
than other competing clouds.

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:26.310
And so the ratio of
18% more for 70% more

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:28.050
power, that's a very good ratio.

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:31.110
We feel like it's a good
value for your money.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:33.510
If you pick Premium, you're
riding on the Google Network,

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.140
which is a non-circuitous
direct private dedicated path

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.960
from your cloud workloads
to your end customers,

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:44.340
egressing out of a POP very
near to your end customers.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.970
If you choose Standard,
you'll benefit from a 6% to 9%

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.580
savings over other
competing clouds.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:53.070
Standard network uses
the public internet,

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:55.320
which is very similar to
what other public clouds will

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.790
provide you.

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:59.700
This is often not a direct path.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.940
It suffers from lower
security, lower liability,

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:05.310
but it's a very good
value for the money.

00:18:08.129 --> 00:18:09.170
Let's talk about storage.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.200
On this graph, on
